Entry 2 in detail (IMPLEMENTED, NOT YET MEASURED)
-------------------------------------------------
Both changes target the two walls entry 0 and entry 1 named. They are in the
tree and verified functionally (GHDL: tb_pe, tb_cgra_top, tb_matvec all pass,
and the host emulator -- the golden reference -- is bit-identical), but they
were written on a machine WITHOUT Vivado, so the Fmax column above honestly
says PENDING rather than carrying a guessed number. Run

    make -f hw.mk fmax STEP_DIV=2      # and STEP_DIV=3

on a machine with Vivado and replace PENDING with what it reports.

(a) rtl/pe.vhd -- MAC in the DSP48E1's native shape.
    Was:  prod := a * b;  res := r + prod(15 downto 0);
    Now:  prod := a * b;  macv := prod + resize(r, 32);  res := macv(15 downto 0);
    Arithmetically IDENTICAL: the low 16 bits of a sum depend only on the low
    16 bits of its addends, so truncating before or after the add gives the
    same answer (tb_cgra_top's MAC case and emu.c both still agree). But the
    second form is P = A*B + C, which a DSP48E1 does inside the tile, so the
    accumulate no longer has to become a fabric CARRY4 hanging off the
    multiplier output -- which is exactly what entry 0's 11-level,
    r_reg->r_reg critical path was made of.

(b) rtl/cgra_ctrl.vhd -- registered PE read-back multiplexer.
    Entry 1's wall was word_idx_reg -> cksum_reg, 8 levels, 5.20 ns: a 16-to-1
    mux over the PE registers AND the checksum adder, in one clock. The mux
    output now lands in rd_word first, so a clock carries the mux or the adder,
    never both. It costs nothing: every consumer (S_RD, S_EX_RD) reaches
    rd_word through at least two states of the transmit handshake, and one UART
    8N1 byte is ~8680 clocks at 100 MHz, so there is no shortage of cycles here.
    The one place that needed care is S_EX_RD's skip path, which advances
    word_idx without sending: it takes an extra settle cycle (rd_settle) so
    rd_word is never read one index behind.

Reading the trajectory
----------------------
STEP_DIV is a clock-vs-throughput knob, not free speed. At N the clock can reach
the point where either the datapath/N or the single-cycle control paths bind,
but each step costs N clocks, so step-rate = Fmax/N:
    N=2 -> 171.4/2 = 85.7 Msteps/s   (datapath-bound: the throughput sweet spot)
    N=3 -> 192.8/3 = 64.3 Msteps/s   (higher CLOCK, lower step-rate)
The fabric is UART-bound in practice, so raw Fclk is headroom + a known ceiling,
not throughput. Pick N=2 for work per second; raise N to prove/where the control
logic tops out.

Next targets (where to go)
--------------------------
* FIRST: measure entry 2. Both of its changes are speculative until `make fmax`
  runs on a Vivado machine. If (a) lands, the N=2 datapath path should drop
  below ~10.5 ns and the wall may move off the datapath entirely; if (b) lands,
  the N>=3 control wall should move off word_idx->cksum onto whatever is next.
  Either way `make fmax` re-names the wall, which is the point of the tool.
* If the datapath is still the N=2 wall after (a): flatten the 16-way op mux.
  The 16 opcodes are a flat case today; grouping them (arith / logic / shift /
  select) so the final selection is a 4-to-1 over pre-reduced results trades
  area for one less LUT level on the tail of the chain.
* If the control path is still the N>=3 wall after (b): the CFG receive side is
  the other 16-way structure (cfg_r write demux). It is a write, not a read, so
  it is cheaper -- but it is the next thing standing.
* Re-run `make fmax` after each change; it re-names the wall and appends to
  build/vivado/fmax_history.csv.

A note on what Fmax buys here
-----------------------------
Nothing, for throughput, until the LINK stops being the bottleneck -- see the
wire-cost table in README.md and Section "Cost and Performance Model" of the
report. The 2026-08-10 protocol work (fused EXEC transaction, config-once scan,
zero-tile skipping) cut round trips by 2-19x depending on the kernel; that is
where the observable speed came from. Fclk remains headroom and a known ceiling,
and this log exists to keep the ceiling measured rather than assumed.
